<p>The <strong>best under desk elliptical</strong> for most people is the Cubii JR2. It is quiet enough for video calls, smooth enough to use for hours and low enough to fit under a standard desk. But "best" depends on your desk height, your budget, your mobility and whether you want to sit or stand, so we ranked eight picks by use case instead of pretending one machine suits everyone.</p>
<p>Every pick below is based on published specs, brand track record and the patterns that show up again and again in owner reviews. We do not quote prices because they change daily; each link takes you to the current listing so you can check the current price.</p>
<p>Short on time? Jump to the <a href="#quick-comparison-of-the-best-under-desk-ellipticals">comparison table</a> or take the quiz further down to get a match in about a minute.</p>

<h2>The Best Under Desk Ellipticals at a Glance</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Best overall:</strong> [[amazon:cubii-jr2]]</li>
<li><strong>Best budget:</strong> [[amazon:sunny-sf-e3872|Sunny Health &amp; Fitness SF-E3872]]</li>
<li><strong>Best for low desks and tall users:</strong> [[amazon:deskcycle-ellipse]]</li>
<li><strong>Best motorized:</strong> [[amazon:ancheer|ANCHEER motorized under desk elliptical]]</li>
<li><strong>Best for seniors and circulation:</strong> [[amazon:legxercise]]</li>
<li><strong>Best for standing:</strong> [[amazon:stamina-inmotion-e1000]]</li>
<li><strong>Best for app tracking:</strong> [[amazon:cubii-jr2-plus|Cubii JR2+ with Bluetooth]]</li>
<li><strong>Best desk bike alternative:</strong> [[amazon:deskcycle-2]]</li>
</ol>

<h2>Best Under Desk Elliptical Picks, Ranked by Use Case</h2>
<h3>1. Best Overall: Cubii JR2</h3>
<p>The Cubii JR2 is the machine most other seated ellipticals get compared to, and for good reason. Its magnetic flywheel is the main thing owners praise: there is no friction pad to wear down or squeak, so it stays quiet months into daily use. The stride is a gentle forward oval that keeps your knees low and moving smoothly rather than pumping up and down.</p>
<p>It has multiple resistance levels, a small display for strides, time and estimated calories, and Cubii sells replacement parts and accessories, which matters more than people expect two years in. The tradeoffs: it is heavier than budget models to carry around, and it is a seated-only machine. If you use a rolling chair, pair it with caster cups so you do not push yourself away from the desk.</p>
[[product:cubii-jr2|1]]

<h3>2. Best Budget: Sunny Health &amp; Fitness SF-E3872</h3>
<p>If you want to find out whether pedaling at your desk sticks before spending more, the Sunny SF-E3872 is the sensible starting point. Sunny is an established home fitness brand, the unit has adjustable resistance, and it is compact enough to slide under a desk or into a closet.</p>
<p>Owners describe it as less refined than a Cubii: the display is basic and the build feels more utilitarian. For light pedaling while you answer email, that hardly matters. Add a mat if your floor is hard or smooth, because lighter machines tend to creep forward. Lighter budget machines also need resistance kept moderate if you are on the heavier side, because the pedals take more stress.</p>
[[product:sunny-sf-e3872|2]]

<h3>3. Best for Low Desks and Tall Users: DeskCycle Ellipse</h3>
<p>Knee-to-desk contact is the single most common reason people return an under desk elliptical. The DeskCycle Ellipse is designed around that problem, with a low pedal height that clears more desks than most competitors. It also has a wide magnetic resistance range, so it works both as background movement and as a real leg workout when you turn it up.</p>
<p>The catch is a larger footprint than mini models, so measure the floor space under your desk as well as the height. Our full <a href="/deskcycle-ellipse-review/">DeskCycle Ellipse review</a> goes deeper on setup and fit.</p>
[[product:deskcycle-ellipse|3]]

<h3>4. Best Motorized: ANCHEER</h3>
<p>A motorized under desk elliptical moves the pedals for you. Switch it on with the remote, choose a speed, and your legs travel through the elliptical path whether or not you push. You can also pedal along for more effort. That makes the ANCHEER a strong choice for people with limited mobility, low stamina or a focus on circulation during long sitting sessions.</p>
<p>Two things to know: it needs a power outlet near your chair, and passive mode burns fewer calories than pedaling against resistance. Motors also add a low hum, so it is not as silent as a magnetic manual model. See all six powered options in our <a href="/best-motorized-under-desk-elliptical/">best motorized under desk elliptical</a> guide.</p>
[[product:ancheer|4]]

<h3>5. Best for Seniors and Circulation: LegXercise Ellipse One</h3>
<p>The LegXercise is a passive leg exerciser first and a workout machine second. Its motor guides your legs through a slow, gentle elliptical motion, which owners in their 70s and 80s often say they can use while watching TV or reading without any strain. It is popular with people who sit for long periods and want to keep their legs moving.</p>
<p>It is not a calorie burner in passive mode, and it needs power. If you are managing a medical condition such as poor circulation, ask your doctor whether a passive exerciser suits you. Our <a href="/best-under-desk-elliptical-for-seniors/">best under desk elliptical for seniors</a> guide covers safety, chair choice and more senior-friendly options. [[button:legxercise]]</p>

<h3>6. Best for Standing: Stamina InMotion E1000</h3>
<p>Most under desk ellipticals are seated-only, and standing on them is a fast way to break a pedal or lose your balance. The Stamina InMotion E1000 is a compact strider built to be used standing, and it also works seated. That makes it the pick for standing-desk owners who want both options.</p>
<p>Expect more noise than a magnetic seated machine, and keep something sturdy to hold for balance when you stand. Read <a href="/can-you-stand-on-an-under-desk-elliptical/">can you stand on an under desk elliptical</a> before you try it on any other model. [[button:stamina-inmotion-e1000]]</p>

<h3>7. Best for App Tracking: Cubii JR2+</h3>
<p>The JR2+ is the same quiet machine as the JR2 with Bluetooth added, so your strides and calories sync to the Cubii app and from there to many fitness platforms. If you are goal-driven and like streaks and charts, that feedback can be what keeps you pedaling past the first month.</p>
<p>If you do not care about the app, the standard JR2 does everything else for less. Wrist trackers often undercount seated pedaling, which is another reason app sync helps. Logging your sessions as a workout fixes that. [[button:cubii-jr2-plus]]</p>

<h3>8. Best Desk Bike Alternative: DeskCycle 2</h3>
<p>Some people want more resistance than a seated elliptical gives. The DeskCycle 2 is an under desk bike with a circular pedal motion, a low pedal height and a wide resistance range, plus a display you can set on your desk. It is quiet and stable.</p>
<p>The circular motion asks more of your knees than an elliptical stride, and your knees rise higher at the top of each turn, so check clearance. If you are torn, our <a href="/under-desk-elliptical-vs-bike/">under desk elliptical vs bike</a> comparison lays out who should pick which. [[button:deskcycle-2]]</p>

